# Copy to /opt/mint-sniper/mint-sniper.env on the VPS. NEVER commit the
# real file — it holds private keys. chmod 600, owned by the mint-sniper
# user, before starting the service (deploy.sh does this for you).
#
# Names here must exactly match what config.toml's private_key_env /
# google_oauth_client_secret_env fields reference — the values round-trip
# nowhere (not to the UI, not to config.toml itself), only the names do.

SNIPER_PK_1=0x_your_wallet_1_private_key_here
SNIPER_PK_2=0x_your_wallet_2_private_key_here
SNIPER_PK_3=0x_your_wallet_3_private_key_here

# Only needed if config.toml's google_oauth_client_id is set (step 10c).
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-google-oauth-client-secret

# Only needed if config.toml's opensea_api_key_env is set (step 8b/8c).
# OPENSEA_API_KEY=your-opensea-api-key

# Only needed if config.toml's mint_execution = "delegated" (delegated
# mint mode, DELEGATED_SERIAL). A single BIP-39 mnemonic phrase — index 0
# is derived as the OPERATOR (the only funded wallet, the only signer);
# indices 1..delegate_count are derived in-memory at fire time as
# RECEIVER addresses only, never written to disk or logged individually.
# Fund ONLY the operator address (see RUNBOOK.md's "Delegated mint mode"
# section for how to derive it from this same phrase in a standard,
# ideally offline wallet tool). Never commit the real value; this name
# must exactly match config.toml's delegate_mnemonic_env field.
# OPERATOR_MNEMONIC=your twelve or twenty four word bip39 mnemonic phrase here
